  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10e-catfishes-from-asia/mystus_leucophasis_formerly_heterobagrus_leucophasis__en/",
      "html": "<p>The Upside-Down-Cat from the Congo (Synodontis nigriventris) is known by any aquarist's child. But even experienced aquarists often don't know that an upside-down swimming species also occurs in Asia (in Burma): Mystus leucophasis, which can attain a maximum length of about 30 cm, but is commonly found 15-20 cm long.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10e-catfishes-from-asia/mystus_leucophasis_formerly_heterobagrus_leucophasis__en/",
      "html": "<p>The schooling instinct in the bustling juveniles, which are currently 4-6 cm long and always quarreling, is that strong that it took a lot of patience to make a photo of a solitary swimming individual.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10e-catfishes-from-asia/mystus_leucophasis_formerly_heterobagrus_leucophasis__en/",
      "html": "<p>Currently the fish are mainly pitch-black; from a length of about 8-10 cm on they develop snow-white spots on the flanks.</p>"
    }
  ],
